ANSER: {
  "rootCause": "The surface preparation procedure update (CA-2024-067) failed because the new solvent cleaning specification was not validated for compatibility with the specific paint primer used, and the operator training did not include practical verification of surface energy levels, resulting in inconsistent surface tension and adhesion failure.",
  "actions": "1. Conduct a DOE (Design of Experiments) to validate the interaction between the specified solvent, the metal substrate, and the paint primer to establish the optimal cleaning parameters. 2. Update the Surface Preparation SOP to include quantitative verification methods (e.g., dyne pen testing or water break test) prior to painting. 3. Implement mandatory 'train-the-trainer' sessions for line supervisors focusing on the new verification methods, followed by competency assessments for all paint line operators. 4. Install automated surface tension monitoring sensors on the paint line to provide real-time data and interlock the paint spray gun if parameters are out of spec.",
  "evidence": "1. Completed DOE report validating the solvent-primer-substrate combination. 2. Revised SOP signed off by Quality and Engineering managers. 3. Training rosters and competency assessment results showing 100% pass rate for operators. 4. Statistical Process Control (SPC) charts showing surface tension data within control limits for 3 consecutive production batches. 5. Zero customer complaints regarding paint adhesion for a period of 6 months post-implementation."
}
